WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:04.000
Let’s say you’re trying to
find some solid information

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.460
about a serious health
problem that concerns you,

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:09.160
like high blood pressure,
or diabetes.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.760
Yet, everywhere you look,

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.480
someone’s trying to sell you
something like vitamins,

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.760
yoga mats, blenders, drugs.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.360
Well, breathe a
sigh of relief

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.480
because all we bring
you are the facts.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.880
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.320
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.280
Did you know that healthy
plant-based diets appear to

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.840
not only reduce the risk
of severe COVID-19,

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.160
but the risk of getting
infected in the first place,

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.200
even independent
of comorbidities.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.320
Here’s our first story.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.880
What’s the role of nutrition for both
preventing and beating COVID-19?

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.400
Nutrition-related myths
on protection and treatment

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.960
have been widely
prevalent in this pandemic,

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.960
and while there is no diet
that completely prevents infection

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.920
there are certainly dietary steps
we can take to minimize risk.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.840
Those with healthier diets
in general, seem to suffer

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:07.760
less serious courses, but this appears
to be an indirect benefit

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.000
of lower rates of obesity
and pre-existing medical conditions.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.000
I mean, that’s great, but is there any diet
that may have direct effects

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.960
on the course of disease
or susceptibility

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:19.040
to infection in the first place?

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.120
Well, plant-based diets
have been shown to decrease

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.960
the risk of suffering from other
respiratory infections—in fact,

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.440
plant-based seminaries
during the pandemic of 1918

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.880
appeared to have been
relatively spared.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.640
Why might a plant-based diet help?

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.720
Vegetable nitrates concentrated
in dark green leafy vegetables

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.480
can be used to make nitric oxide,

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:40.000
which is part of our lungs’ first line
of defense against infection.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.480
There are also polyphenol
phytonutrients in plants

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.240
that may directly inhibit
viral infection.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.800
Then there are beneficial
effects on our microbiome,

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.960
which plays a key role in our
system-wide immune function.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.760
Fiber-containing foods facilitate
more of a symbiotic relationship

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:57.240
with our gut bugs, whereas
processed junk and animal foods

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.840
can have the opposite,
dysbiotic effect.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.800
Fiber, concentrated
in whole plant foods,

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.280
is the single most anti-inflammatory
food component,

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.280
whereas saturated fat, concentrated
in meat, dairy, and junk,

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.640
is the most pro-inflammatory
food component.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.680
And so, no surprise
those consuming

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.120
more pro-inflammatory diets

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.440
were at greater risk
of suffering a severe course,

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.520
and of getting COVID-19
in the first place,

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.400
up to nearly
twelve times the odds,

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.680
even after taking
into account body weight,

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.880
diabetes, and blood pressure.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.880
But this study only covered
60 COVID patients.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.000
How about 11,000 COVID patients?

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.840
Diet-associated
inﬂammation increased

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.000
the risk of getting
COVID-19 infection,

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.600
suffering a severe course
and dying from the disease.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.760
Might this help explain
why the COVID death rates

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.960
were a hundred times
lower in Africa?

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:44.640
Can a plant-based diet
help mitigate

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.880
COVID-19? Let’s find out.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:51.720
In this study of COVID-19 illness
severity in the elderly

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.880
in relation to vegetarian versus
non-vegetarian diets, statistically,

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.920
the nonvegetarians had up to
like 20 times the odds

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.640
of suffering a critically
severe course of COVID,

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:04.710
but that was based
on only nine vegetarians.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.040
Now here, a study of thousands
of front-line healthcare workers

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.720
across six countries found that
those eating plant-based diets

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.800
had 73 percent lower odds
of suffering a moderate

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.520
or severe course
of COVID-19,

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.600
and even combined
with pescatarians

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:21.200
(who eat fish but no other meat)
still had significantly lower odds.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.960
The biggest spread was between
those eating plant-based

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:25.440
versus low-carb.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:26.960
Compared to plant-based
healthcare workers,

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:29.200
those eating low carb,
high-protein diets had nearly

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.320
four times greater odds of suffering
a serious infection.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:35.480
This may not seem
surprising given that many

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:38.960
of the underlying risk factors
for COVID-19 mortality—obesity,

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.360
heart disease, high blood pressure,
and type 2 diabetes—

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.920
can be controlled or even
reversed with a healthy enough

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.760
plant-based diet and lifestyle.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:50.000
But the apparent plant-based
protection went above and beyond.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.040
The greatly reduced risk was
independent of all those conditions.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.400
The researchers suggest
that a "healthy diet

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.720
rich in nutrient-dense foods
may be considered for protection

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:02.200
against severe COVID-19."

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.440
The one food group most associated

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:08.040
with lower symptom severity
in one outpatient study was legumes,

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.520
followed by grains, the top sources
of dietary fiber.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.000
The investigators suggest
that the anti-inflammatory effects

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.000
of the short-chain fatty acids
released by our fiber-feeding

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.480
good gut bugs may explain
the connection.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:23.720
The healthcare worker study
enrolled a few thousand people,

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:25.680
but Harvard researchers
collected data

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.800
from nearly 600,000 participants
using a plant-based diet

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:33.120
scoring system where you get
points for eating healthy plant foods,

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.520
but points deducted for meat,
eggs, junk, and dairy.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:38.160
And indeed those scoring
higher not only had

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:41.880
a significantly lower risk of suffering
a severe course of COVID-19,

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:43.600
but also a significantly lower risk

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:45.840
of getting infected
in the first place.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:49.320
And again, this was after taking
comorbidities into account,

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:52.600
alongside nondietary risk factors
such as exercise and smoking.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:56.200
The bottom line: "the best
available evidence suggests

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.320
that plant-based diets are beneﬁcial
in directly reducing the risk

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:02.280
of severe COVID-19 symptoms
and the risk of infection,"

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.480
even independent of other
health-related factors.

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:08.400
A more Mediterranean-style
diet may reduce the risk

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.000
of infection by 22 percent,

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:11.880
while an even more
plant-based diet

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:15.320
may cut infection risk in half.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:19.280
For dealing with long COVID, a plant-
based diet has been suggested

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:22.160
based on the abundance of data
showing positive associations

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.720
of plant-based diets
with improved immune function,

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.320
neurotransmitter balance, pain
and inﬂammation reduction,

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:29.120
improved sleep, and mental health.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.920
So, in terms of lifestyle adjustments
in long-COVID management,

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:35.440
"large-scale promotion
of plant-based eating"

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.480
may have the potential to improve
physical and mental impacts,

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:41.480
but this has yet
to be put to the test.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.360
There was a study that suggested
that those who eat more vegetables

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.000
are less likely to end up depressed,

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.240
and those who eat more nuts,
beans, and whole grains

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:51.360
may end up less depressed, anxious,
and stressed after COVID-19,

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.600
whereas stress levels were
correlated with higher meat intake.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.880
But the only interventional
studies are like this,

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.440
showing a detrimental impact
on high-fat high-sugar diets,

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:02.880
but that’s in Syrian hamsters,

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.560
though there are definitely
some unhappy hamster lungs

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:07.600
on the crappy diet.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.080
All in all, adopting
a healthy plant-based diet

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:13.400
may be a powerful tool to decrease
the risk of severe COVID-19

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.280
and should be promoted as
a public health safety measure.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:18.080
And what are the side-effects?

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:20.680
A delay in aging
and decreased disease.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:23.800
Maybe this is the booster we need.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.600
After all, if real effort
had gone into encouraging

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:29.160
and incentivizing healthier diets
and lifestyles,

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:32.280
we would not only have saved
many more lives from COVID-19,

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:34.560
but we would also reduce
future mortality

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:39.120
from all major causes of death.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:39.840
Finally today,

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.280
I share with you some research
about how to reform the food system

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:46.400
before it’s too late.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:50.760
Infectious diseases are emerging
globally at an unprecedented rate.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:54.360
Literally hundreds of new pathogens
have emerged and re-remerged

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:58.400
over the last few decades and
what we eat is responsible

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:03.520
for most of the new diseases that
have jumped from animals to humans.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:08.920
In response to the torrent of emerging
zoonotic or animal-to-human diseases,

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:11.960
three of the world’s leading authorities—
the World Health Organization,

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:14.600
the Food and Agriculture Organization
of the United Nations,

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:17.520
and the World Organization
for Animal Health,

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:22.040
held a joint consultation to
determine the key underlying causes.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:26.920
First on their list was the “Increasing
demand for animal protein.”

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:30.200
The greatest swords of Damocles dangling

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:34.760
are the H5 and H7 bird flu viruses
blanketing much of the earth.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:37.800
A bird flu pandemic could be devastating

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:42.520
given their current upwards of
Ebola-like flip-of-the-coin death rates.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:45.240
Given that the emergence of
these deadly bird flu viruses,

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:50.360
H5N1 and H7N9 are “linked to
intensification of the poultry sector,”

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:54.960
there have been calls for the
“de-industrialization of animal production,”

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.080
for example, as suggested
here in the Annals of the

00:07:57.080 --> 00:08:00.560
New York Academy of Sciences,
“replacing large industrial units

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.400
with smaller farms with
lower stocking densities,”

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:07.000
potentially resulting in less stress,
less disease susceptibility,

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:12.360
less intense infectious contact,
and smaller infectious loads.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:16.600
Maybe they’re the ones that could
use a little social distancing.

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:20.240
The American Public Health Association,
the largest and oldest association

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:25.040
of public health professionals in
the world, has called for a moratorium

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:28.640
on factory farming for
nearly two decades now.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:33.440
Maybe COVID-19 is the dry run we
needed, the fire drill to awake us

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:39.680
from our complacency and reform
the food system before it’s too late.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:43.160
But if, as the Food and Agriculture
Organization of the United Nations

00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:45.800
visualized it, the next
pandemic starts with

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:49.120
“increased demand for poultry
products,” before ending up

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:52.600
with human-to-human transmission,
maybe we need to move beyond

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:55.360
just giving these animals
some more breathing room.

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:59.320
It is curious, therefore,
given the pandemic threat,

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:01.920
that changing the way
humans treat animals—

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:05.800
most basically, ceasing to
eat them or, at the very least,

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:08.720
radically limiting the quantity
of them that are eaten—

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:13.360
is largely off the radar as a
significant preventive measure.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:16.600
Such a change, if sufficiently
adopted or imposed,

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:21.000
could still reduce the chances
of the much-feared influenza epidemic.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:22.560
It would be even more likely to prevent

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:25.240
unknown future diseases that,
in the absence of this change,

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:27.320
may result from farming animals

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:29.280
intensively and killing them for food.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:33.760
Yet, humanity doesn’t
even consider this option.

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:39.080
However, thanks to food
innovations, this may be changing.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:42.680
Have you looked in the dairy
case at the supermarket lately?

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:45.960
Some of America’s largest
dairy producers have recently

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:52.760
filed for bankruptcy due to the
constellation of new consumer choices.

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:57.880
I was peripherally involved in the
largest meat recall in human history.

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:00.960
Remember the footage of
the cows getting forklifted?

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:04.160
A hidden-camera investigation at
a California slaughter plant

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:10.040
for “spent” dairy cows led to a
recall of nearly 150 million pounds

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:13.000
of beef for violations of food
safety rules meant to protect

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:15.360
the public from mad cow disease.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:18.480
Downed dairy cows, too sick to even walk,

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:20.600
were being dragged to
slaughter with chains

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:23.520
into the federal school lunch program.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.800
You don’t have to worry about
contaminated cattle brains...

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:29.040
in your oatmilk, though.

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:32.960
Plant-based milks are a no-brainer.

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:34.360
But, you can see what I’m saying.

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:37.760
Yes, you can pass public
health regulations to stop

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:41.240
the cannibalistic feeding of
slaughterhouse waste to dairy cows

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:45.520
—or you can just provide the public
better alternatives and let

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:51.960
the market eliminate the risk entirely
because there’s no prions in plants.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:56.720
HIV/AIDS likely arose from
people slaughtering primates.

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:59.960
30 million people wouldn’t
be dead right now

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:05.960
if we were eating meals from
bushes instead of bushmeat.

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:08.520
We can’t get coronaviruses
from cauliflower.

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:11.440
There is no flu in falafel
production no matter

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:14.520
how tightly you crowd the balls together.

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:16.640
What I'm saying, is our food choices

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:20.720
don’t just affect our personal
health, but our global health.

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:25.040
Not just in terms of climate change,
but in terms of stifling pandemic risk.

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:29.560
There's been a tremendous surge in
interest in diversified protein sources,

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:33.080
given the increasing consensus
that reduced meat consumption

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:35.880
is critical for addressing
both the climate crisis

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:39.320
and our burgeoning epidemics
of lifestyle diseases.

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:43.080
Eating less meat may not
only help save the world,

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:44.280
but could help prevent the loss of

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:48.080
more than ten million
human lives a year.

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:51.160
To their credit, in 2016, the
Chinese government recommended

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:54.600
its citizens cut their meat
consumption in half in part

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:57.560
to reduce their growing
rates of chronic disease.

00:11:57.560 --> 00:12:03.400
A completely plant-based
diet might reap $30 trillion

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:06.920
from the health benefits alone,
and that would be just

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:10.360
from the lowered rates of chronic
diseases like cancer, heart disease,

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:12.960
type 2 diabetes, not even factoring in

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:16.520
the decreased catastrophic pandemic risk.

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:19.760
What we eat doesn’t just
affect our personal health,

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:23.480
but our global health in
more ways than one.

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:28.200
Making healthier choices could also help
mediate the next coronavirus epidemic,

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:31.400
not only at the source by
sidestepping wet markets,

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:35.120
but by also decreasing the rates
of comorbidities found to increase

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:37.480
the risk in all the deadly coronaviruses:

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:41.840
SARS, MERS, and COVID-19.

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:46.400
Consider the underlying risk factors
for COVID-19 severity and death—

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:50.840
obesity, heart disease,
hypertension, type 2 diabetes

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:52.880
and chronic pulmonary disease.

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:57.520
In virtually all studies, vegetable
protein is superior to animal protein

00:12:57.520 --> 00:13:00.720
in terms of lower rates of heart
disease, and type 2 diabetes

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:03.720
and lower blood pressure
than animal protein.

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:07.400
The same diet that can help
you survive this pandemic

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:10.160
can also help prevent the next.

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:13.200
So instead of propping up the
meat industry to the tune

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:18.040
a 100 million taxpayer dollars
a month, and forcing meat plants

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:23.240
to stay open,
pandemic proof your diet.

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:28.000
Thankfully, expanded options are
now hitting the meat case as well.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:32.560
No longer a niche market for
vegetarians, major meat producers

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:36.920
have started blending in vegetable
proteins to make hybrid meat products

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:43.000
like Perdue’s chicken plus nuggets
or Tyson’s “Whole Blends” sausage links.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:45.840
Smithfield, the world’s
largest pork producer,

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:49.680
recently debuted an entire line
of plant-based products.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:54.960
Hormel, has a new plant-based line
too, the purveyors of SPAM…

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:58.800
now believe in the power of plants.

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:01.560
Check out this headline
from a few weeks ago:

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:07.840
“KFC to roll out Cargill’s plant‐
based chicken across China.”

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:10.760
Talk about a cultural revolution.

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:13.080
And, we’re not talking
about Tofurkey.

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:16.960
Cargill is America’s largest
private corporation,

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:20.400
and one of the biggest meat
packers in the world.

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:24.560
How many fewer curly-tailed
viral mixing vessels are there now

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:27.840
that Dunkin’ Donuts has a
meat-free breakfast sausage?

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:32.080
How many fewer hens are packed beak-
to-beak now that egg-free mayo

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:35.000
has taken the sandwich
spread sector by a storm?

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:38.400
Quorn, a brand of meat-free meat
made from the mushroom kingdom,

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:40.840
opened a single facility
that can produce the meat

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:44.840
equivalent of twenty
million chickens per year.

00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:48.080
These products may not be the
healthiest from a personal standpoint—

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:53.480
a doughnut sandwich without
pork is still a doughnut sandwich—

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:57.040
but hey, swap in an egg-free
omelette from Tim Hortons

00:14:57.040 --> 00:14:58.680
and from a pandemic standpoint?

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:01.480
Zero risk.

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:04.604
Doesn’t necessarily have
to be plants, though.

00:15:04.664 --> 00:15:08.320
Researchers are seeking
alternative protein sources everywhere.

00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:13.000
Can’t think of any possible
alternative to cow’s milk?

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:15.960
How about cockroach milk?

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:19.640
Could be healthier than cow’s
milk, and hey no lactose,

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:22.280
no dairy allergy problems.

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:24.480
An important alternative.

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:27.400
I mean I can’t imagine anything
else you can make milk out of.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:30.200
And gluten-free too!

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:33.280
The only downside,
evidently, was the flavor,

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:36.840
but the researchers—
perhaps funded by Big Bug?—

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:41.040
chalked this up to the fact that the
judges knew there were cockroaches

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:44.240
in the bread; and so,
they were all just biased.

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:48.040
Hmmm... I think I’ll stick with the plants.

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:51.400
But if you’re like: “you’ll
have to pry that pork chop

00:15:51.400 --> 00:15:54.080
from my cold, dead hands,”

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:57.280
we may be able to have
our meat and eat it too.

00:15:57.280 --> 00:16:00.200
An even more innovative
approach to pandemic prevention

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:04.800
was suggested by
Winston Churchill in 1932.

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:07.160
In an article in Popular
Mechanics entitled

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:09.560
“Fifty Years Hence,”
he predicted that

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:11.600
“We shall escape the
absurdity of growing

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:14.640
a whole chicken in order
to eat the breast or wing,

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:18.680
by growing these parts separately
under a suitable medium.”

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:22.200
And indeed, the prediction
is becoming a reality.

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:25.760
Instead of taking a cutting from
a plant and growing vegetables

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:28.520
you’re taking a sample from
an animal and growing meat.

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:34.520
Potentially lots of meat, like maybe
a billion pounds from a single sample.

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:38.040
Indeed, in terms of efficiency,
growing meat straight from muscle cells

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:42.820
could reduce greenhouse gas emissions
and water use by as much as 96%,

00:16:42.820 --> 00:16:49.440
and lower land use by as much as 99%,
but when you factor in pandemic risk,

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:52.800
the benefits to human health
of a slaughter-free harvest

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:56.640
could arguably rival
those to planetary health.

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:58.840
Food safety has been
considered the primary

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:00.920
human health benefit
of such an approach.

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:03.320
There has been a six-fold
increase in food poisoning

00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:06.440
over the last few decades, sickening
tens of millions of Americans every year,

00:17:06.440 --> 00:17:10.080
and contaminated meats and animal
products are the most common cause.

00:17:10.080 --> 00:17:15.480
So, when the cultivated meat industry
calls its product “clean meat,”

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:18.400
that’s not just a nod to clean energy.

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:21.160
Food-poisoning pathogens
like E. coli, Campylobacter,

00:17:21.160 --> 00:17:24.400
and Salmonella are
due to fecal residue,

00:17:24.400 --> 00:17:27.920
traces of which are found on most
poultry sampled in the United States

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:31.160
and about half of retail
ground beef and pork chops.

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:34.880
They’re intestinal bugs; so, you
don’t have to worry about them

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:37.960
if you’re producing meat
without intestines.

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:43.120
You don’t have to cook the crap out of
meat, if there’s no crap to begin with,

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:47.320
just like you don’t have to worry
about brewing up new respiratory viruses

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:52.960
that could kill millions of people if
you’re making meat without the lungs.

00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:58.040
“A culinary choice in south China
led to a fatal infection in Hong Kong,

00:17:58.040 --> 00:18:02.360
and subsequently to 8000 cases of
severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS),

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:06.760
and nearly 1000 deaths in 30
countries on six continents.”

00:18:06.760 --> 00:18:10.560
If only we had learned our lesson then.

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:13.760
We may be one bushmeat meal away

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:17.800
from the next HIV, one
pangolin plate away

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:22.680
from the next killer coronavirus,
and one factory farm away

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:25.440
from the next deadly flu.

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:28.400
Tragically, it may take a
pandemic with a virus

00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:35.000
like H5N1 before the world realizes
the true cost of cheap chicken.

